hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well one of the issues  that inevitably arises for any  religiously oriented philosopher is the  relationship between some sort of faith  whether we understand that in terms of  lived faith or an experience or  adherence to certain scriptures or  doctrines and the Faculty of human  reason and often these are viewed as  opposed to each other or there's a neat  dividing line between them for st.  Augustine in on free choice of the will  it's not going to be quite so  antagonistic a relationship and in fact  what we're going to see is the the way  in which he conceptualizes it along with  his student who is following along with  them of odious is that the - you might  say reinforce each other and and help  each other out so there isn't this very  common presumption of something either  being of faith or of reason and these  being two wholly separate modes that  might be you know super ordinated in  some way so that faith is over reason or  reason is over faith instead they they  they cooperate with each other  in ways that we'll see here so to begin  with early on in the work already in  Chapter four  Augustine says when his student is  asking him about a very difficult  question about evil Augustine says  you're inquiring into a problem which  deeply interested me when I was quite a  young man it troubled me so much I was  worn out and driven right into heresy  you can find that in his confessions  solo did I fall etc etc if God had not  helped me because I longed to find the  truth I could not have escaped or  recovered the primary freedom to search  so what he's saying there is that God in  some way aids the human Enquirer in  seeking out the truth the theme that  we'll come back to momentarily what he  goes on then and he says I will explain  it it's you and the way I finally worked  out God will help us and make us here's  the key idea understand what we believe  so two important terms there belief or  believe in credit a in Latin and you  know sometimes it's used in in other  ways  credo means I believe when we talk about  having a Creed it's coming from that and  then understanding in tell it again a  right to be able to literally apply our  intellect to it and make sense of it now  a lot of people would say well these are  two different you know modes of  apprehension one of them is deficient in  relation to the other you might think  about Plato for example talking about  you know grasping something with  knowledge with a piston a or through  noose or do noia  and then having mere opinion Doakes ah  right having it could be write opinion  but it's merely believing something not  actually knowing it an Augustine brings  up this this passage that he uses quite  frequently coming from the book of  Isaiah the Septuagint version  Augustine gives priority to the Greek  versions of the text rather than the  Hebrew versions of the text and the  Greek Septuagint there in fact is a  passage that translated into Latin goes  nisi credit or ETS known and 10 agates  meaning unless you believe you will not  understand and so the idea here is you  have to have some sort of starting point  for the inquiry you have to believe in  something that doesn't mean that you  dogmatically or unquestioningly or  blindly believe in it because the very  fact of putting it out there is  something that you believe then  inevitably leads to trying to go beyond  the mere belief and to understand but  unless you do in fact have some sort of  commitment according to Augustine at  least a temporary commitment of belief  you're not going to arrive at  understanding if you dictate that  understanding has to come first and only  after that we can believe you'll never  according to him get the process started  so he says we can be sure that we're  treading in the paths pointed out by the  prophet Isaiah right we believe and then  he says what is it we actually believe  everything which create is which exists  is created by one God that God is not  the cause of sin so then how do we trace  back this this you know causality so  that God is not responsible for sin  notice that belief here is not what it  is for many people in the past and in  the present a way of closing a question  a way of blocking the path to inquiry  and study says Wow  believing in some things really raises  some problems for us that then we are  called to go beyond the mere belief and  figure out this is what makes it  Christian philosophy and not just say  believing in Christianity and then then  proceeding on there's an attempt to try  to provide an intelligibility to it to  understand and I think another really  key aspect of this that you see coming  up over and over again in this work and  in other Augustinian works you  is this notion that God will assist God  will help out in some way God will  contribute not by like giving you the  the answer automatically or just popping  it into your head but in some way by by  lending some strength or you know  guiding slightly here and there or  giving some confidence that's needed in  order to make some progress so God  assists The Enquirer or in this case two  inquires Augustine and his student 