aluations and again how  do we understand the eternal law we we  don't just believe that it's there we  try to understand it another key thing  that's being clarified here and it's on  free choice of the will is the scope of  the will what is the will what does it  do as it turns out the will is in fact  involved in wrongdoing when it chooses  something other than following the  eternal law or choosing you know  temporal goods over higher goods or when  it chooses to abandon the Justice it has  in its own will all of these things are  being worked out within this framework  of you know which can be understood in  terms of unless you believe you will not  understand first belief comes and belief  is not the end of the matter  it's not a unshakable foundation as you  know will come about in later  foundationalism no it's a starting point  that provides some initial  intelligibility and then the question is  as human beings endowed with a Faculty  of reason how much more intelligibility  can we provide to these matters provided  we seek truth and ask for divine help so  that's Augustine's view in a nutshell on  the relations between Christian faith  and human reason  in this this particular work  you
 

